   "takeover_note": "No first-visit claim window: HestiaCP's installer is CLI-only (hst-install-ubuntu.sh) and it creates the admin itself with v-add-user, so unlike CloudPanel there is nothing for a first visitor to claim.  The admin password exists from install time, so the class is secret, not claim.  Verified in the source 2026-11-19 (hestiacp/hestiacp @ 1.10.8): (1) HestiaCP's password handling is BETTER than VestaCP's on both counts that matter - the interactive installer DOES ask for the admin password (read -p 'Please enter administrator password: ', with a validate_password loop) instead of never asking, and the generated fallback comes from gen_pass() which reads /dev/urandom for 16 characters from [A-Za-z0-9] (~95 bits) rather than bash RANDOM for ten (~32 bits); (2) the residual is the -p flag, which takes the admin password on the command line - an automated install (cloud image, provisioning script, VPS template) can hardcode it, and a template with a fixed password is permanent mass compromise rather than a window; that is the takeover signal worth hunting, and it is measurable as a cluster (one password across many panel URLs); (3) there is no forced password change on first login; (4) the password-reset mail is sound - the reset code is 12 characters of a bcrypt salt and the mail's hostname comes from the configured hostname, NOT from the Host header, so VestaCP's host-header reset trick (CVE-2020-10966) has no counterpart here; (5) the pre-auth mail-password endpoint is IP-restricted to the host's own addresses and refuses requests carrying forwarded-IP headers; (6) fail2ban is installed with a Hestia jail and the API needs either the root password or a 192-bit access key, so SPRAYING IS A POOR BET here.",
