   "needs_list": true,
   "needs_list_reason": "population is the ZoomEye title=\"Vesta\" count (227,612), a CONTAINS match on the panel's <title>Vesta - Login</title> - its marker precision was never measured, so the figure is a candidate total, not a population.  Needs a host list and a probe of the marker (one GET per host, read the title and the /images/vesta_logo.png reference).  ZoomEye is currently returning 402 credits_insufficient, so neither the census nor the probe could be run today.  Precise markers for when the credits are back: http.body=\"vesta_logo.png\", title=\"Vesta - Login\", title==\"Vesta - Login\", or http.header.server=\"nginx\" && port=8083.",
   "population": 9817,
   "measured_by": "title",
   "rce_path": "VestaCP is a MULTI-TENANT hosting panel whose PHP front end shells out to root-run v-* scripts, and the panel's own php-fpm pool runs as the system user 'admin', which has NOPASSWD sudo on /usr/local/vesta/bin/* - so any code execution inside the panel is root.  THE KEY PATH IS NOT THE PANEL FRONT DOOR, IT IS THE STORAGE FORMAT: per-account objects (cron jobs, FTP users, DNS records, mail accounts) are stored as KEY='value' lines in $USER_DATA/*.conf, and func/main.sh parses them with 'eval \"$object\"' / 'eval $line' inside every root-run v-* script (get_object_value, update_object_value, search_objects, sync_cron_jobs).  The quoting escape is the %quote% placeholder, so a value that reaches a conf file with a raw quote breaks out of the quoting the next time a root script parses it - that is the tenant-to-host escape documented as GHSA-m2mc-xwq7-pw72 / VESTACP-2026-001 (2026-10-06, CVSS 9.9, no CVE id, no upstream fix), and the sink is STILL PRESENT in the newest release v1.0.0-10 (verified in the source 2026-11-19).  THE CONCRETE 2-REQUEST CHAIN (inherited from HestiaCP's July-2026 fixes, never applied to VestaCP): the file manager creates directories with no filename validation (v-add-fs-directory checks only path containment), so a tenant can plant a directory named x'$(cmd)' and then point the domain's FTP account at it - v-change-web-domain-ftp-path validates containment but not character content and writes the value with update_object_value, which escapes backslash, ampersand and slash but NOT the single quote, so web.conf ends up holding a broken FTP_PATH='...' line.  The tenant then triggers the parse with GET /search/?q=... : web/search/index.php routes a non-admin to v-search-user-object, which greps web.conf/dns.conf/mail.conf/db.conf/cron.conf and runs eval on each matching line AS ROOT (bin/v-search-user-object:112).  Root code execution.  HestiaCP fixed this class as CVE-2026-84981 /-84984 (web.conf fields, v-search-user-object) and CVE-2026-84979 (newline injection in v-add-cron-job; VestaCP's outer eval has no sandbox at all).  VestaCP has the FTP_PATH vector and the cron vector, but NOT the CUSTOM_DOCROOT one (no v-change-web-domain-docroot in 0.9.8-27 or v1.0.0-10).  BUILT-IN, NO-CVE PATHS once you hold one ordinary tenant account: (a) add a cron job - v-add-cron-job stores CMD='<command>' in $USER_DATA/cron.conf and sync_cron_jobs evals it as root; (b) the file manager writes into a domain's public_html, where the domain's php-fpm executes it; (c) phpMyAdmin is served at http://<panel-host>/phpmyadmin/ and is linked straight from the panel's database page; (d) the /api/v1 surface runs any v-* command as root once you have a user API key.  NO PRE-AUTH PATH EXISTS: every NO_AUTH_REQUIRED endpoint was enumerated from the source and checked for writes - the pre-auth password-reset path writes only server-generated values (RKEY and the hex MD5) into user.conf while the attacker-chosen password goes to /etc/shadow, the reset/mail endpoint only accepts the server's own IP and the configured NAT IPs, and both upload handlers are session-gated - so no unauthenticated request can plant a value in a conf file, which means no pre-auth route to the eval sink.  UNAUTHENTICATED-ADJACENT (no unauthenticated RCE verified): CVE-2020-10966 - the pre-auth password-reset endpoint puts $_SERVER['HTTP_HOST'] in the reset mail, so a Host-header injection captures the reset code and gives an account takeover (one victim click); CVE-2020-10808 - the backup-listing command injection (EPSS 77.5%, the highest of any VestaCP bug) is second-order: plant a crafted filename, then any panel action fires it; CVE-2021-43693 - the v_language local file inclusion is scored 9.8/PR:N by NVD, but the source gates it behind $_SESSION['user']=='admin' (the same sink is reachable through the API with a user key), so it is admin-level, not pre-auth.  FINAL FILTER (unauthenticated + zero click): NONE.  Every path in the map needs either a panel account or one panel action; the host-header reset needs a victim click; the LFI is admin-gated despite its PR:N score; and the only way into the conf files that feed the eval sink is a root-run v-* script invoked under the session user's identity, which no unauthenticated request can reach (all five NO_AUTH_REQUIRED endpoints write only server-generated values).  The panel therefore has no unauthenticated zero-click RCE path - and that is a structural result, not an absence of looking.  WHERE ZERO-CLICK WOULD LIVE IS THE HOST STACK, NOT VESTACP: the stock firewall opens 3306 (MySQL), 5432 (PostgreSQL), 21, 25, 110, 143, 8080, 8433 and 8083 to the world, and the box carries exim, dovecot, roundcube (/roundcube, /webmail), phpMyAdmin (/phpmyadmin) and vsftpd/proftpd.  Pre-auth RCE candidates there are the exim series, dovecot before 2.3.8 (CVE-2020-12100) and the world-open database ports - all of them measurement tasks, since the versions are whatever the distro image shipped.  proftpd's CVE-2015-3306 (mod_copy) is RULED OUT: the shipped config loads only mod_vroot, mod_auth_pam and mod_auth_unix.  SPRAYING IS A POOR BET here: fail2ban's vesta-iptables jail is enabled by default with maxretry=5 and every failure mode (web login, API hash check, API key check) logs 'failed to login', so five attempts get you iptables-banned, and there is no default password to spray.  Workspace: /root/VestaCP-RCE-2026t/docs/VESTACP.md",
   "cves": "CVE-2020-10808, CVE-2020-10966, CVE-2020-10786, CVE-2020-10787, CVE-2019-9859, CVE-2019-12791, CVE-2019-12792, CVE-2021-28379, CVE-2021-30462, CVE-2021-30463, CVE-2021-43693, CVE-2021-46850, CVE-2021-47873, CVE-2020-36948, CVE-2022-3967, CVE-2022-34025, CVE-2022-36303, CVE-2022-36304, CVE-2022-36305, CVE-2018-1000884, CVE-2018-18547, CVE-2018-25117, CVE-2018-10686, CVE-2019-9841, CVE-2015-2861, CVE-2015-4117, GHSA-m2mc-xwq7-pw72 (VESTACP-2026-001, eval sinks -> root, no CVE id)",
